The conditional operator (?:) is also called the ternary operator in JavaScript because it requires three operands.

Syntax: var variablename = (condition) ? value1 : value2;

How it works: The condition is evaluated first. If the condition is true, value1 is assigned to the variable. If the condition is false, value2 is assigned.

Example:
var result = (10 > 15) ? 100 : 150;
// Since 10 > 15 is false, result = 150

var age = 20;
var status = (age >= 18) ? 'Adult' : 'Minor';
// status = 'Adult' because 20 >= 18 is true

The Prompt dialog box is used when the programmer wants to pop up a text box to get user input. It enables the user to interact with the program by entering a value.

Syntax: prompt('Message', 'defaultValue');
or: window.prompt('sometext', 'defaultText');

Parameters: (i) A label/message to display above the text box; (ii) A default string to pre-fill in the text box.

Buttons: OK — returns the text entered in the box; Cancel — returns null.

Example:
var sname = prompt('Please enter your name', 'Name');
// Shows a text box with the message and 'Name' pre-filled

JavaScript has three basic data types:

1. String: A list of characters. String literals are indicated by enclosing characters in single quotes or double quotes. Strings can contain single or multiple characters, whitespace, and special characters (\n = newline). Example: var name = 'Raman'; or var msg = "Hello World";

2. Number: Can be integer (whole number) or floating-point (decimal) value. Numeric literals are written directly as numbers. Example: var age = 18; var pi = 3.14;

3. Boolean: Can only be one of two values: true or false. Boolean literals are written as true or false directly in source code. Example: var isLoggedIn = true;

JavaScript is dynamically typed (untyped) — no need to declare the data type when declaring a variable. The same variable can hold different data types at different times.

JavaScript supports three types of popup (dialog) boxes:

1. Alert Dialog Box:
Used to give warning messages to users. Shows a message and provides only one button: OK. When the user clicks OK, the dialog closes and execution continues.

Syntax: alert('Message'); or window.alert('Message');

Example:
var value1 = 522;
window.alert('Data1: ' + value1);

Use case: Warn users that a required field is empty.

2. Confirm Dialog Box:
Used to get the user's consent or confirmation. Shows a message with two buttons: OK (returns true) and Cancel (returns false). The return value can be used to determine what action to take.

Syntax: confirm('message'); or window.confirm('message');

Example:
var choice = confirm('Do you want to continue?');
if (choice) { // user clicked OK }
else { // user clicked Cancel }

Use case: Confirm a delete action before proceeding.

3. Prompt Dialog Box:
Used to get user input via a text box. Has two parameters: (i) label text, (ii) default string. Two buttons: OK (returns entered text) and Cancel (returns null).

Syntax: prompt('Message', 'defaultValue'); or window.prompt('text', 'default');

Example:
var sname = prompt('Please enter your name', 'Name');
document.write('Hello, ' + sname);

Use case: Ask the user for their name or any input before processing.

JavaScript supports all basic arithmetic operators:

1. Addition (+): var sum = 20 + 120; // sum = 140

2. Subtraction (-): var diff = 20 - 120; // diff = -100

3. Multiplication (*): var prod = 10 * 100; // prod = 1000

4. Division (/): var res = 100 / 522; // res = 5.22 (approximately)

5. Modulus (%) - remainder: var rem = 100 % 522; // rem = 22

Shorthand Operators:
+=, -=, *=, /=, %=
Example: var sum = 120; sum += 20; // sum = 140 (same as sum = sum + 20)

JavaScript Program using Arithmetic Operators:
<script language='javascript' type='text/javascript'>
var value1 = 522, value2 = 10;
document.write('Data1: ' + value1);
document.write('<br>Data2: ' + value2);
var sum = value1 + value2;
var diff = value1 - value2;
var prod = value1 * value2;
var res = value1 / value2;
var rem = value1 % value2;
document.write('<br>Sum: ' + sum);
document.write('<br>Difference: ' + diff);
document.write('<br>Product: ' + prod);
document.write('<br>Division: ' + res);
document.write('<br>Remainder: ' + rem);
</script>

Output: Sum=532, Difference=512, Product=5220, Division=52.2, Remainder=2
